# Walkthrough: Database Migration to Docker

I have successfully migrated your database setup from a local PostgreSQL instance to the Docker-based setup. This ensures that you and your teammates are always in sync and simplifies the collaboration flow.

## Changes Made

### 1. Data Synchronization
- Initialized `docker/init.sql` with the latest data from your local `role_navigator` database using the [./refresh-docker-db.sh](file:///Users/mohammedriyaz/Downloads/TX_NEW_PROMPT/Role-Navigator/refresh-docker-db.sh) script.
- Restarted Docker containers with a fresh volume to ensure the database was initialized from the updated `init.sql`.

### 2. Configuration Update
- Modified [.env](file:///Users/mohammedriyaz/Downloads/TX_NEW_PROMPT/Role-Navigator/.env) to point to the Docker database:
  ```env
  DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5435/role_navigator
  ```

### 3. Cleanup & Automation
- Verified that the Docker database is running and contains the expected tables (`roles`, `use_cases`).

## Verification Results

### Database Connection
I verified the connection to the new Docker database using `psql`:
```bash
psql postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5435/role_navigator -c "\dt"
```
**Result:**
```text
           List of relations
 Schema |   Name    | Type  |  Owner   
--------+-----------+-------+----------
 public | roles     | table | postgres
 public | use_cases | table | postgres
```

### Application Server
I attempted to start the server via `npm run dev`. It reported that port `5000` is already in use, which likely means your existing server process is still running. It will now automatically use the new `DATABASE_URL` upon its next restart or if it reloads [.env](file:///Users/mohammedriyaz/Downloads/TX_NEW_PROMPT/Role-Navigator/.env) changes.

## Confirmed Collaboration Workflow

Yes, you are **exactly right**! The workflow is now standardized for both you and your teammate:

1.  **When you make changes to the DB**:
    - Run [./refresh-docker-db.sh](file:///Users/mohammedriyaz/Downloads/TX_NEW_PROMPT/Role-Navigator/refresh-docker-db.sh).
    - This will now automatically extract data from your **Docker container** and update `docker/init.sql`.
    - Commit and push the updated `docker/init.sql`.

2.  **When your teammate makes changes**:
    - You `git pull` the latest changes.
    - Run [./user-load-db.sh](file:///Users/mohammedriyaz/Downloads/TX_NEW_PROMPT/Role-Navigator/user-load-db.sh) (or manually run `docker compose down -v && docker compose up -d`).
    - This will reset your Docker database using the new `docker/init.sql` from Git.

## Updated Helper Scripts

I have updated your scripts to ensure they target the Docker container instead of your local (non-Docker) PostgreSQL:

- **[refresh-docker-db.sh](file:///Users/mohammedriyaz/Downloads/TX_NEW_PROMPT/Role-Navigator/refresh-docker-db.sh)**: Now uses `docker exec` to dump data directly from the container. Both you and your teammate can now use this same script.
- **[user-load-db.sh](file:///Users/mohammedriyaz/Downloads/TX_NEW_PROMPT/Role-Navigator/user-load-db.sh)**: Now acts as a shortcut for resetting your Docker volume (`docker compose down -v && docker compose up -d`).

> [!TIP]
> Since you are now using the Docker database on port **5435**, if you use any external tools (like PGAdmin or DBeaver), make sure to update your connection string to use port `5435` and the password `postgres`.
